1
00:00:00,653 --> 00:00:08,245
Archiving puts a record aside without changing its identity or description. Its
existing link remains useful.

2
00:00:08,245 --> 00:00:13,465
This demonstration uses synthetic collection data and a real disposable database.

3
00:00:14,893 --> 00:00:21,698
Archive record opens a confirmation naming this record. It explains that browsing and
search will no

4
00:00:21,698 --> 00:00:28,503
longer show it, and restoring it to browsing is currently unavailable. Cancel closes
the confirmation without

5
00:00:28,503 --> 00:00:30,204
sending an archive request.

6
00:00:31,617 --> 00:00:38,607
Another signed-in session corrects the name while this confirmation is open. The old
confirmation cannot archive

7
00:00:38,607 --> 00:00:44,724
the changed record. Workbench loads the current saved details and requires a new
decision.

8
00:00:46,137 --> 00:00:54,113
After a new confirmation, the archive commits, but we deliberately interrupt its
response. Workbench says the

9
00:00:54,113 --> 00:01:02,090
outcome could not be confirmed. Changing appearance preserves the recovery controls.
Retry sends the same checked

10
00:01:02,090 --> 00:01:02,588
version.

11
00:01:04,021 --> 00:01:11,119
The retry discovers the current archived state. The record is read-only at the same
address. Archiving

12
00:01:11,119 --> 00:01:18,218
has preserved its description and storage location. This state does not claim which
request archived it.

13
00:01:19,628 --> 00:01:25,631
The archived record no longer appears in collection search, in either Grid or List.
Returning through

14
00:01:25,631 --> 00:01:31,633
its saved link still shows the archived record. Archiving does not delete the record
or assert

15
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:35,010
that the physical item was sold or disposed of.
